---
// Banking, from the one angle that is verifiable and nobody writes about.
//
// Account-opening timelines are folklore and change by bank and by month,
// so this page does not trade in them. What is written down and stable is
// the scope line of the Consumer Protection Regulation: it covers natural
// persons and sole proprietorships, and says in terms that a sole
// proprietorship is not an LLC. Incorporating moves you out of it.
import VerticalLayout from "../../layouts/VerticalLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@dst/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Faq from "@dst/ui/Faq.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "Does the UAE consumer protection regime cover my company's bank account?",
    a: "Not if the company is an LLC. The Consumer Protection Regulation C 8/2020 applies to financial products and services provided to consumers that are natural persons and sole proprietorships. The regulation states expressly that a sole proprietorship is not a limited liability company.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is a sole proprietorship, for this purpose?",
    a: "A legal entity for commercial or professional activities owned and operated by a natural person, who is in complete control of operations and profits and bears personal unlimited liability. The unlimited liability is the price of staying inside the protective regime.",
  },
  {
    q: "Who supervises banks in the UAE?",
    a: "The Central Bank of the UAE, under Decretal Federal Law No. 14 of 2018 regarding the Central Bank and organisation of licensed financial institutions and activities. The Consumer Protection Regulation was issued under Article 121 of that law and has been in force since 25 December 2020.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the company's activity need Central Bank approval?",
    a: "If the activity is financial. Financial institutions, banks, money exchanges, insurance companies and insurance agents need the Central Bank's approval as part of licensing, on top of the economic department's own process.",
  },
];
---
<VerticalLayout
  vertical="llc"
  current="/llc/banking/"
  prose
  title="Business banking in Dubai: what an LLC is not owed"
  description="The UAE's Consumer Protection Regulation covers natural persons and sole proprietorships — and says an LLC is neither. What that means for a Dubai company's banking relationship."
  image="/covers/llc-banking.jpg"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Banking"
    title="Business banking in Dubai: what an LLC is not owed"
    image="/covers/llc-banking.jpg"
    imageAlt="Cut-paper illustration: a flat wall crossing the frame with one narrow doorway cut through it, and a small upright figure standing in the opening."
    imageKind="generated"
  >
    <p class="lede">
      The UAE has a detailed consumer protection regime for financial
      services, in force since <strong>25 December 2020</strong>. It covers
      natural persons and sole proprietorships, and it states in terms that a
      sole proprietorship is not a limited liability company. Incorporating
      an LLC therefore moves the banking relationship out of that regime and
      into contract — which is the single most useful thing to know before
      the first meeting, and the thing no setup brochure mentions.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Where the protective line actually falls</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>Consumer Protection Regulation C 8/2020</strong> was issued
      by the Central Bank under Article 121 of
      <strong>Decretal Federal Law No. 14 of 2018</strong>. It applies to all
      licensed financial institutions in their provision of financial
      products and services to consumers that are
      <strong>natural persons and sole proprietorships</strong>. A
      "consumer", in its own definition, is any natural person or sole
      proprietor obtaining financial services to satisfy their own or
      others' needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the regulation defines a sole proprietorship as an entity owned and
      operated by a natural person who controls operations and profits and
      bears <strong>personal unlimited liability</strong> — adding, for
      clarity, that it is not a limited liability company. The protection and
      the unlimited liability travel together. Limited liability is bought
      with the protection, which is a trade most founders make without ever
      being told it is one.
    </p>

    <h2>What that means in the room</h2>
    <p>
      For an incorporated company, the terms of the account are the terms of
      the contract the bank offers. Fee changes, notice periods, complaint
      handling and disclosure standards that a regulated consumer could point
      to are, for an LLC, whatever the agreement says. This is not a warning
      that banks behave badly; it is a statement about where to look when
      something goes wrong. The answer is the mandate and the schedule of
      charges, and they are worth reading at signing rather than at dispute.
    </p>

    <h2>When the Central Bank is in the setup path itself</h2>
    <p>
      Most companies meet the Central Bank only as their bank's regulator.
      Companies whose own activity is financial meet it earlier: financial
      institutions, banks, money exchanges, insurance companies and insurance
      agents need Central Bank approval as one of the additional government
      approvals in the licensing sequence, before the economic department
      issues anything. That approval sits on top of the
      <a href="/llc/registration/" title="Registering a company in Dubai, step by step">nine-step route</a>,
      not inside it, and it is the step that sets the timeline.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Callout kind="warn">
    <p>
      Nothing here is a comment on how long an account takes to open. That
      figure moves by bank, by activity and by quarter, and any number
      published in advance is somebody's average rather than your answer. Ask
      the bank, in writing, and hold the written answer.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Before the first appointment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The trade licence and the attested Memorandum of Association, which the licensing route has already produced.</li>
      <li>A registered physical address — <strong>Ejari</strong> in Dubai — because the bank will ask for the same lease the economic department did.</li>
      <li>Clarity on which activities on the licence the account will actually be used for.</li>
      <li>The company's credit file, which is a separate question and is on <a href="/llc/financing/" title="Financing a Dubai company once it trades">the financing page</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqItems} />

  <section class="container">
    <p class="source-note">
      Scope, the definitions of consumer and sole proprietorship, the
      enabling law and the commencement date: the Central Bank of the UAE
      Rulebook, Consumer Protection Regulation C 8/2020, read 15 September
      2026. Central Bank approval among licensing approvals: the Official
      Platform of the UAE Government, steps to start a business on the
      mainland, read the same day. Nothing here is legal or financial advice.
    </p>
  </section>
</VerticalLayout>
